## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates real-world stateful Kafka Streams processing with a TTL-backed side-input, focusing on correctness under cache miss, update, and eviction scenarios, as well as robust testing strategies. It also assesses the ability to design APIs and observability for per-user enrichment in a high-throughput pipeline.

## Key Concepts

- TTL-based caching and eviction semantics
- Side-input-driven cache updates
- Per-message offset guarantees and at-least-once delivery
- Idempotent emits and deduplication

## Code Example

```java
// Implementation sketch
class FraudScoringSink implements KafkaSink {
  private final LoadingCache<String, RiskProfile> cache;

  public FraudScoringSink() {
    this.cache = Caffeine.newBuilder()
      .expireAfterWrite(10, TimeUnit.MINUTES)
      .build(CacheLoader.from(this::fetchFromStore));
  }

  @StreamListener
  public void onTransaction(Transaction txn) {
    RiskProfile profile = cache.getIfPresent(txn.userId());
    if (profile == null) {
      profile = RiskProfile.unknown();
    }
    emit(txn.enrich(profile));
  }

  @StreamListener
  public void onFraudSignal(FraudSignal signal) {
    cache.put(signal.userId(), signal.toProfile());
  }
}
```

## Testing Strategy

```java
@Test
void cacheMissEmitsUnknownAndReEnrichesOnSignal() {
  store.onTransaction(tx("u1", 100));
  verify(emit).calledWith(risk="unknown");

  store.onFraudSignal(signal("u1", 0.9, "velocity"));
  verify(emit).calledWith(risk="high");
}

@Test
void ttlEvictionClearsStaleRiskData() {
  cache.put("u1", profile(0.8));
  clock.advance(11, MINUTES);
  cache.cleanUp();
  assertNull(cache.getIfPresent("u1"));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to implement a pragmatic validation gate with dead-letter queue (DLQ) routing in KCNA, along with careful offset management to preserve per-message semantics in a multi-partition, multi-topic environment. It also tests understanding of canary test design for validating end-to-end behavior in production-like scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Per-message schema validation in KCNA pipelines
- Dead-letter routing and sink isolation for failed records
- Offset management: commit-after-acknowledge pattern for reliability
- Canary test design with mixed valid and invalid records

## Code Example

```javascript
// KCNA validation gate
function validateSchema(msg) {
  const v = msg.value;
  if (!v || typeof v.userId !== 'string' || typeof v.eventType !== 'string' || typeof v.timestamp !== 'number') {
    return { valid: false, error: 'Schema validation failed' };
  }
  return { valid: true };
}

// Process each message
async function processMessage(msg, { producer, commitOffsets }) {
  const validation = validateSchema(msg);

  if (validation.valid) {
    await producer.send({ topic: 'events-validated', messages: [msg] });
    await commitOffsets([{ topic: 'events', partition: msg.partition, offset: msg.offset + 1 }]);
  } else {
    await producer.send({ topic: 'dead_letter', messages: [{ value: { original: msg.value, error: validation.error } }] });
    await commitOffsets([{ topic: 'events', partition: msg.partition, offset: msg.offset + 1 }]);
  }
}

// Canary test scenario
const testRecords = [
  { topic: 'events', partition: 0, value: { userId: 'u123', eventType: 'click', timestamp: 1700000000 } },
  { topic: 'events', partition: 0, value: { userId: 'u456', eventType: 'view' } },
  { topic: 'events', partition: 1, value: { userId: 'u789', eventType: 'purchase', timestamp: 1700000001 } },
];
```
